body:
Summary:
This PRD proposes an opinionated SDK surface and runtime defaults for delivering partial/ incremental structured results over streaming responses while enforcing tool-calling safety and providing telemetry-driven defaults. The goal is to give product teams a low-effort, high-safety integration path for server and client SDKs that reduces common implementation mistakes, improves developer ergonomics, and leads to predictable operational behavior (latency, cost, and safety).

Problem statement / context:
- Customers want both low-latency partial results and strongly-typed, machine-readable final outputs (JSON/records) for downstream systems (search indexing, agents, ticket creation).
- Current hosted-api drafts cover many streaming contract designs but leave SDK ergonomics and safety policies underspecified; teams reimplement ad-hoc partial-parsing, retries, and tool call validation.
- Frequent issues observed in integration projects: silent parsing errors on partial frames, unvalidated tool calls executed by orchestration layers, cascading retries that blow up token cost, and lack of observability around partial-to-final output reconciliation.

Goals:
1) Define an SDK surface (server and browser) that prioritizes safe tool execution by default and provides clear primitives for partial structured results.
2) Provide runtime defaults (backpressure, buffer windows, max-latency-to-first-structured-frame) that balance developer expectations and SLOs.
3) Emit standard telemetry for partial/frame reconciliation, parse-fail rates, tool-call rejections, and fallback activations.
4) Deliver a rollout plan with staged opt-in and migration helpers for existing customers.

Non-goals:
- Replacing all streaming contract formats (we will be opinionated and provide one recommended path).
- Deep changes to server-side model generation strategy; this PRD focuses on SDK ergonomics and runtime policy defaults.

High-level approach / proposal:
1) Partial Structured Frames (PSF): introduce a lightweight frame type that the runtime will surface over streaming: three frame kinds - TEXT_DELTA, STRUCTURED_DELTA, STRUCTURED_COMMIT.
   - TEXT_DELTA: existing token deltas for fallback human display.
   - STRUCTURED_DELTA: incremental, append-only JSON Patch-like fragments (line-delimited JSON snippets representing subtrees). Each STDelta must be idempotent if replayed and include a monotonic 'frame_index'.
   - STRUCTURED_COMMIT: final validated structured payload with signature (hash of combined frames) indicating reconciliation point.
2) SDK primitives (opinionated defaults):
   - onTextDelta(callback) -> for rendering progressive text.
   - onStructuredDelta(callback) -> incremental structured payload pieces. SDK will buffer and apply patches with automatic merge and validate schema (if provided).
   - onStructuredCommit(callback) -> final reconciled payload or schema validation error.
   - registerToolContract(name, schema, validator) -> registers allowed tool signatures and validators; tool calls emitted by models must be validated before runtime dispatch.
   - executeToolSafely(name, payload, opts) -> wrapper that enforces rate limits, idempotency id, and pre-flight validation.
3) Safety-first tool policy:
   - Default: all tool-calls from models are deliver-to-host but not auto-executed. SDK exposes an explicit acceptToolCall(toolCallId) that a developer must call to trigger execution.
   - Policy options: auto-accept (opt-in per-route), manual-accept (default), sandboxed-exec (execute in a constrained environment).
   - All tool-call payloads must pass registered schema validators; any deviation raises a 'tool_call_rejection' event emitted to telemetry and surfaces a structured error to client callbacks.
4) Reconciliation and fallback behavior:
   - If STRUCTURED_COMMIT signature mismatches the client-reconstructed state from STRUCTURED_DELTA frames, SDK will: (a) attempt up to N small replays to reconcile (b) if still mismatched, surface structured_error and present TEXT fallback to consumer.
   - Configurable window: buffer_duration_ms (default 300ms) that groups near-simultaneous frames before delivering to app callback to reduce churn for UI components.
5) Backpressure and latency SLOs used as defaults:
   - Default max_outstanding_frames = 5. If exceeded, runtime sends a BACKPRESSURE frame advising model to prioritize commit-rate. SDK surface exposes shouldThrottle() to apps so they can apply client-side local backpressure.

SDK ergonomics examples (pseudocode):
// register expected structured schema for 'issue.create' responses
client.registerSchema('issue.create', issueSchemaJson);
client.registerToolContract('http.post', { url: 'string', body: 'object' }, validatorHttpPost);

client.onStructuredDelta((frame) => {
  // incremental apply, UI-friendly
  renderPartialCard(frame.patch);
});

client.onStructuredCommit((payload, meta) => {
  if (meta.valid) {
    // safe to persist/run downstream
    persistRecord(payload);
  } else {
    // show human fallback and emit event for telemetry
    showFallbackUI(payload.textFallback);
  }
});

client.onToolCall(async (call) => {
  // default behaviour: manual acceptance
  if (shouldAutoAccept(call)) {
    const res = await client.executeToolSafely(call.name, call.args);
    client.respondToToolCall(call.id, res);
  } else {
    surfaceToolCallToOperator(call);
  }
});

Telemetry and observability:
- Emit standard events with counts and sample payloads: structured_delta_received, structured_commit_validated, structured_commit_mismatch, structured_parse_failure, tool_call_rejected, tool_call_executed, tool_call_timeout, client_backpressure_triggered.
- Recommended tags for events: route, model, route-tier, schema-id, tool-name, region.
- Provide an out-of-the-box dashboard in Console: partial-frame throughput, parse error rate, median commit-latency, tool-call acceptance rate, and average cost-per-commit token.

Compatibility and migration notes:
- Backwards compatibility: existing clients expecting pure token streaming will continue to work because TEXT_DELTA frames are always emitted. SDK default to TEXT streaming only unless client calls registerSchema or enables structured mode.
- Migration helper: a server-side flag 'emit_structured_preview' to mirror structured deltas in a non-breaking manner for integration testing.

Rollout plan (phased):
- Phase 0 (internal): implement SDK primitives and server support behind feature flag, run internal dogfood on support workflows and ticket ingestion. (2 sprints)
- Phase 1 (alpha customers): opt-in via API flag, collect telemetry, tweak defaults (buffer window, outstanding frames). (4 weeks)
- Phase 2 (beta): add Console dashboards and allow per-tenant safety policy toggles. (6 weeks)
- Phase 3 (GA): enable structured mode as an officially supported contract, provide migration guides and example integrations (Zendesk, Ops agents). (8–12 weeks)

Success metrics:
- Developer integration time for structured outputs drops from baseline by 40% (survey + time-to-first-success).
- Structured parse-failure rate <= 0.5% for GA model surfaces.
- Tool-call unintended-execution incidents = 0 post-rollback window (measured per month) for opt-in customers with manual accept default.
- 75th percentile commit-latency <= 350ms for simple schemas under typical load for Hosted tier.

Open questions / tradeoffs:
- How complex can STRUCTURED_DELTA fragments be before we hit client-side CPU/network limits? Tradeoff: smaller patches increase message count but reduce rework.
- Should we require server-signed STRUCTURED_COMMIT signatures for all official models (adds complexity but improves attack resistance)?
- How aggressively should runtime drop partial frames under overload vs. deliver degraded text fallback?
- Nice-to-have: a visual diff tool in Console to inspect delta application across frames.

Implementation notes (engineering constraints):
- Runtime: minimal additions to streaming protocol (label frames, include frame_index and commit_hash). Avoid changing tokenization path.
- SDKs: supply reference implementations in JS/TS (browser + node) and Python. Keep server-side proxy behavior equivalent across SDKs.
- Security: require validators to run in sandboxed environment for enterprise private deployments; provide a mock validator harness for local dev.

Appendix: example frame sequence (toy):
1) TEXT_DELTA: "User: create ticket about login"
2) STRUCTURED_DELTA(frame_index=1): "{"ops":[{"path":"/title","value":"Login fails on iOS"}]}"
3) STRUCTURED_DELTA(frame_index=2): "{"ops":[{"path":"/body","value":"App crashes after tapping login btn (stacktrace...)"}]}"
4) STRUCTURED_COMMIT(hash=abc123): "{"id":"TK-1001","title":"Login fails on iOS","body":"App crashes ..."}"

Concluding note:
This PRD favors an "opt-in structured" default that keeps text-first ergonomics while enabling teams to adopt a safer, deterministic path for machine-to-machine integrations. The opinionated defaults aim to reduce common pitfalls (unvalidated tool execution, partial parsing errors, runaway retries) while providing clear telemetry and migration steps for customers.

Next steps:
- Product: agree on defaults for buffer_duration_ms, max_outstanding_frames, and default tool policy (propose: 300ms, 5 frames, manual-accept).
- Eng: prototype JS SDK and runtime frame labeling in a feature-flagged build; provide internal test harness.
- DRI: Ava Martinez (product), Rohan Patel (runtime lead), Priya Desai (SDK).
